// J-Link Commander script: flash the firmware and run it.
//
// Used by `cmake --build build --target flash-jlink`, and by
// `PROBE=jlink ./build.sh flash`.
//
// Comments here MUST use `//`. J-Link Commander does not treat `#` as a comment
// -- it echoes each such line back as "Unknown command", and with -ExitOnError 1
// the first one aborts the script before it reaches `connect`. Verified against
// V9.72: of `//`, `/*` and `;` all are accepted, `#` is not.
//
// @par Why a script file rather than -c options
// JLinkExe has no equivalent of OpenOCD's -c: it reads commands from stdin or
// from a -CommanderScript file. A file also keeps the settings that must match
// the debug configuration in one reviewable place, the way openocd_dap.cfg does
// for the CMSIS-DAP path.

// Device name as it appears in J-Link's device library. Verified accepted by
// JLinkGDBServer V9.72 -- a wrong name is reported as "unknown device" rather
// than being ignored. The same string appears in .vscode/launch.json; changing
// one means changing both.
device STM32H723VG

// SWD, not JTAG: this board exposes only SWCLK/SWDIO.
if SWD

// 4000 kHz, matching openocd_dap.cfg's `adapter speed 4000` so the two probe
// paths behave the same. J-Link tolerates more on a short cable, but a rate the
// wiring cannot sustain shows up as intermittent CRC errors rather than as
// anything naming the clock.
speed 4000

connect

// The ELF, not the .hex. loadfile takes either, and the ELF is what the build
// gates and what the debugger loads symbols from -- flashing the same artifact
// removes one way for the image on the target to disagree with the one being
// debugged.
//
// @LOADFILE@ is substituted by CMake with the ELF's absolute path (see
// configure_file in CMakeLists.txt) -- which is why the file J-Link actually
// reads is build/flash.jlink, not this template. A path relative to the working
// directory cannot work: the build target runs with cwd = build/, so
// `build/...elf` does not resolve there. J-Link's own %CD% does not help either
// -- on Linux V9.72 it is passed through literally, and the failure is a bare
// "Failed to open file" that never says what path was tried.
loadfile @LOADFILE@

// Reset and run, so `flash` leaves the board doing its job rather than halted.
r
g

// Close without prompting; without this JLinkExe waits for input and the build
// target never finishes.
qc
